\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Warp spacetimes are usually studied \emph{metric-first}: one prescribes $g_{\mu\nu}$, reads off $T_{\mu\nu}=G_{\mu\nu}/8\pi$, and analyzes frozen slices~\cite{helmerich2024warpfactory}. Such matter is rarely obtained from a consistent initial-value problem and need not survive evolution. Here ``FTL'' means an \emph{effective} null shortcut relative to flat space between the same asymptotic endpoints---never local superluminal motion outside the light cone.

We invert this: the optimizer proposes matter; \texttt{GRTresna} returns compatible initial data; \texttt{GRTeclyn} evolves it; probes ask whether a gauge-invariant null shortcut emerges and survives falsification.

\subsection{Methodological Rigor}
\label{subsec:rigor}

Three pillars distinguish this work from metric-first studies:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Initial value problem:} constraint-clean data from physical scalar sources, not coordinate scripts.
    \item \textbf{Dynamic feedback:} full BSSN/CCZ4 evolution; dispersing matter collapses the channel.
    \item \textbf{4D geodesic falsification:} the headline metric is \texttt{ftl\_geo\_evolving}---null rays integrated through the \emph{time-evolving} metric stack. Frozen-slice geodesics can over- or under-state transport (positive control \texttt{eval\,144} vs.\ negative control \texttt{eval\,086}).
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Contributions}
\label{subsec:contributions}

\begin{itemize}
    \item[(i)] End-to-end pipeline with matched QD/CMA-ES objective and HQ 4D verify (Secs.~\ref{sec:pipeline}--\ref{sec:methodology}).
    \item[(ii)] Candidate \texttt{eval\,144}: verified $\sim\!8\%$ 4D null shortcut at HQ resolution (Sec.~\ref{sec:results}).
    \item[(iii)] Honest limits: transient precursor, exotic matter, late-time trapping---not passenger transport (Sec.~\ref{sec:discussion}).
\end{itemize}

\section{Matter-First Pipeline}
\label{sec:pipeline}

\subsection{Search Loop}
\label{sec:loop}

$\theta \rightarrow$ initial data $\rightarrow$ \texttt{GRTeclyn} $\rightarrow$ diagnostics $\rightarrow S(\theta)$. Scalar sources [Eq.~\eqref{eq:scalar_sources}] feed \texttt{GRTresna}; the score combines 4D shortcut terms, health (constraints, survival), and risk (exotic/energy conditions). In the \texttt{ftl\_4d} campaign the headline channel is \texttt{ftl\_geo\_evolving} from the 4D tracer; frozen geodesic and coordinate FTL are gated off until the trace completes.

\begin{equation}
\rho=\tfrac12\psi^{-4}|\tilde\nabla\phi|^2+\tfrac12\Pi^2+V(\phi),
\qquad
S_i=-\Pi\,\partial_i\phi ,
\label{eq:scalar_sources}
\end{equation}

\subsection{Shell Parameterization}
\label{sec:shell}

A low-dimensional shell vector defines scalar clouds on a Fibonacci sphere with searchable orientation, toroidal/poloidal/radial currents, exotic wedge, and mass/coupling. MAP-Elites preserves diverse mechanism families across an $8\times8$ archive ($\texttt{ftl\_lifetime}$ descriptor).

\subsection{Self-Consistency}
\label{sec:selfconsistent}

Matched signed-scalar evolution, mass-matched potential, and a post-load gate ($\|H\|_{L^2},\|M\|_{L^2}\lesssim10^{-2}$) reject inconsistent \texttt{.gridinit} files before GPU evolution.

\subsection{Three-Stage Search (\texttt{ftl\_4d})}
\label{sec:stages}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Stage 1 --- MAP-Elites QD} (\texttt{ftl\_4d\_v1}): $N=128$, $\mathrm{ml}=2$, $t=16$, $\sim\!192$ evals, 4D \emph{search} geodesic, \texttt{ftl\_first}. Best: \texttt{eval\,156}, score $508$.
    \item \textbf{Stage 2 --- CMA-ES} (\texttt{ftl\_4d\_cmaes\_v1}): warm-start from QD top-5, $\sigma_0=0.05$, $144$ evals, same objective. Winner: \texttt{eval\,144}, score $596$ ($+17\%$ vs.\ QD).
    \item \textbf{Stage 3 --- HQ falsification}: fresh \texttt{GRTresna} + GPU at $N=256$, $\mathrm{ml}=3$, $t=30$, 4D \emph{hq} trace, frames, incremental scoring.
\end{itemize}

