\subsection{What moves faster than light}

Nothing physical moves superluminally. Scalar clouds and local light cones remain sub-luminal; coordinate $\max c\approx1.31$ is down-gated as delocalized coordinate offset in scoring. The $\sim\!8\%$ figure is a \emph{null-geodesic timing advantage} through the warped region relative to flat space---the same class of effect as in Alcubierre-type metrics, but here from evolved matter fields and certified by 4D integration through the full history.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\subsection{Interpretation}

\texttt{eval\,144} demonstrates that matter-first search with 4D falsification can discover self-consistent spacetimes whose \emph{honest} null transport gain survives HQ promotion. Methodologically, the pairing with \texttt{eval\,086} establishes when frozen geodesics fail. Physically, the candidate is a \textbf{transient warp precursor}: exotic matter ($\texttt{exotic\_penalty}=-1.6$), dynamic bubble ($\texttt{structural\_persistence}\approx0.63$), corroborated late-time trapping ($t\approx29$) though horizon penalty suppressed---not a persistent corridor or transport vehicle.

\subsection{Applications and next steps}

The pipeline supports (i)~inverse design of null-transport under energy-condition penalties, (ii)~QD atlases of mechanism families, and (iii)~Pareto maps of shortcut vs.\ exotic cost vs.\ persistence. Priorities: raise persistence without losing 4D $f_{\rm geo}$, world-tube objectives, and non-dispersing matter models (e.g.\ boson stars).

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We presented a matter-first discovery pipeline with MAP-Elites, CMA-ES, and HQ 4D null-geodesic falsification. Candidate \texttt{eval\,144}---a dynamic five-lump exotic shell---yields a verified end-to-end shortcut $f_{\rm geo}=7.96\%$ at HQ resolution, matching search-tier measurement. Frozen mid-run geodesics peak higher but collapse; the 4D tracer is essential. We claim a reproducible, falsifiable numerical program for warp-precursor spacetimes, not a functional warp drive.

